      "content": "Apologies for the confusion! I meant to use \"goblins\" as a playful metaphor to describe the elusive nature of the pests you're dealing with, not to imply there's anything supernatural. I understand that can be a bit confusing. Let's get back to the practical advice.\n\nIt sounds like you might be dealing with tomato leaf miners, which are indeed a common pest for tomato plants. These pests are small, white or yellowish insects that lay their eggs on the underside of the leaves. The larvae then burrow into the leaves, creating the tiny holes you've noticed. They can be quite difficult to spot because they tend to stay hidden within the leaves.\n\nTo manage this pest, you can try a few different methods:\n\n1. Remove and destroy affected leaves: Cut out the leaves with the holes and dispose of them properly. This can help reduce the population of leaf miners in your greenhouse.\n\n2. Introduce natural predators: Ladybugs, lacewings, and predatory mites are natural enemies of tomato leaf miners. You can purchase these beneficial insects and release them into your greenhouse to help control the pest population.\n\n3. Apply insecticidal soap or neem oil: These organic insecticides can help kill the leaf miners without harming your plants. Be",
